//? Title: Distance correlation
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Measure dependence of any form between random vectors of any dimension with distance covariance, which is zero only under independence, and test it by permutation

Pearson correlation sees only linear association: \(Y=X^2\) with symmetric \(X\) has correlation zero. **Distance covariance** (Székely, Rizzo and Bakirov 2007) compares the joint characteristic function with the product of the marginals, and \(\mathcal V^2(X,Y)=0\) if and only if \(X\) and \(Y\) are independent (given finite first moments). Its sample version uses only pairwise distances. Let \(a_{ij}=\lVert x_i-x_j\rVert\), with \(b_{ij}\) defined likewise for \(y\), and let \(A\) and \(B\) be the double-centred matrices (row, column and grand means removed). Then

\[\mathcal V_n^2=\frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}B_{ij},\qquad \mathcal R_n=\frac{\mathcal V_n(X,Y)}{\sqrt{\mathcal V_n(X,X)\,\mathcal V_n(Y,Y)}}\in[0,1].\]

\(\mathcal R_n=1\) only for similarity transformations (\(y=a+bCx\) with \(C\) orthogonal), and \(x\) and \(y\) may have different dimensions. The V-statistic is biased upward, increasingly so in high dimension. The U-centred version gives an unbiased \(\Omega_n\) and a **bias-corrected** \(\mathcal R^*_n\). With \(M=n(n-3)/2\), the statistic \(T=\sqrt{M-1}\,\mathcal R^*_n/\sqrt{1-\mathcal R^{*2}_n}\) is approximately \(t_{M-1}\) under independence when the dimensions are large (Székely and Rizzo 2013). The **permutation test** of \(n\mathcal V_n^2\), which shuffles \(y\) against \(x\), is exact at any dimension. **Partial distance correlation** removes the dependence on a third vector \(z\) in the U-centred Hilbert space.

`distance_correlation` returns all of these, with an optional permutation test and the distance exponent `index` \(\in(0,2)\). `partial_distance_correlation` gives the partial version. The tests reproduce `energy` 1.7.12 (`dcov.test` estimates and \(n\mathcal V_n^2\) for three exponents, `bcdcor`, `dcorT.test`, `dcovU` and `pdcor`) to \(10^{-11}\), and its permutation p-values within Monte Carlo error.

The example compares Pearson, Spearman and distance correlation on six relationships with n = 100, averaging estimates and rejection rates over 500 samples each. The simulations estimate the size of each test under independence, including high dimension, where the t test is meant to work, and the power for linear and quadratic signals as noise grows.

```{cpp, id=dc, file="figures/distance_correlation.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/distance_correlation.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/partial_correlation.hpp>

#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using dm::stats::PartialMethod;
using Matrix = dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>;

// Relationship k with noise level s: returns (x, y).
std::pair<std::vector<double>, std::vector<double>> draw(dm::random::SplitMix64& rng, int k,
                                                         std::size_t n, double s) {
    std::vector<double> x(n), y(n);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        const double u = 2 * rng.uniform01() - 1, e = rng.normal();
        switch (k) {
        case 0: x[i] = u, y[i] = u + s * e; break;                          // linear
        case 1: x[i] = u, y[i] = u * u + s * e; break;                      // quadratic
        case 2: {                                                           // circle
            const double a = 2 * std::numbers::pi * rng.uniform01();
            x[i] = std::cos(a) + s * rng.normal(), y[i] = std::sin(a) + s * e;
            break;
        }
        case 3: x[i] = u, y[i] = std::sin(4 * std::numbers::pi * u) + s * e; break;  // sine
        case 4: x[i] = u, y[i] = (0.1 + std::abs(u)) * e; break;            // variance only
        default: x[i] = u, y[i] = e;                                        // independent
        }
    }
    return {x, y};
}

int main() {
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(23);
    const char* names[6] = {"Linear", "Quadratic", "Circle", "Sine (2 periods)",
                            "Variance depends on x", "Independent"};
    const double noise[6] = {0.5, 0.1, 0.05, 0.3, 0, 0};
    std::println("| Relationship (n = 100) | Mean Pearson r | Mean dCor | "
                 "Mean bias-corrected dCor | Pearson power | Spearman power | "
                 "dCor permutation power | dCor t power |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (int k = 0; k < 6; ++k) {
        const int reps = 500;
        double r = 0, dc = 0, bc = 0, pw[4] = {0, 0, 0, 0};
        for (int rep = 0; rep < reps; ++rep) {
            const auto [x, y] = draw(rng, k, 100, noise[k]);
            const auto p = dm::stats::partial_correlation(x, y, {});
            const dm::stats::PartialOptions ranks{.method = PartialMethod::spearman};
            const auto s = dm::stats::partial_correlation(x, y, {}, ranks);
            const auto d = dm::stats::distance_correlation(
                x, y, {.permutations = 199, .seed = static_cast<std::uint64_t>(rep + 1)});
            r += p.estimate / reps, dc += d.dcor / reps, bc += d.dcor_bias_corrected / reps;
            pw[0] += p.p_value <= 0.05, pw[1] += s.p_value <= 0.05;
            pw[2] += d.permutation_p_value <= 0.05, pw[3] += d.t_p_value <= 0.05;
        }
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | "
                     "{:.3f} |", names[k], r, dc, bc, pw[0] / reps, pw[1] / reps,
                     pw[2] / reps, pw[3] / reps);
    }

    // Size under independence: univariate n = 30, and p = q = 50 with n = 30.
    std::println("\n| Independent data | Mean dCor | Mean bias-corrected dCor | "
                 "Permutation test size | t test size |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const std::size_t dim : {1uz, 50uz}) {
        const int reps = 1000;
        double mean = 0, mean_bc = 0, perm = 0, t = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
            Matrix x(30, dim, 0.0), y(30, dim, 0.0);
            for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 30; ++i)
                for (std::size_t j = 0; j < dim; ++j)
                    x(i, j) = rng.normal(), y(i, j) = rng.normal();
            const auto d = dm::stats::distance_correlation(
                x, y, {.permutations = 199, .seed = static_cast<std::uint64_t>(r + 1)});
            mean += d.dcor / reps, mean_bc += d.dcor_bias_corrected / reps;
            perm += (d.permutation_p_value <= 0.05) / double(reps);
            t += (d.t_p_value <= 0.05) / double(reps);
        }
        std::println("| n = 30, dimension {} each | {:.3f} | {:.4f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                     dim, mean, mean_bc, perm, t);
    }

    // Power against noise for linear and quadratic signals (n = 50, 5% level).
    const std::vector<double> levels{0.1, 0.2, 0.35, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0};
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> power(4, std::vector<double>(levels.size(), 0.0));
    const int reps = 300;
    for (std::size_t l = 0; l < levels.size(); ++l)
        for (int k = 0; k < 2; ++k)
            for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
                const auto [x, y] = draw(rng, k, 50, levels[l]);
                const double pp = dm::stats::partial_correlation(x, y, {}).p_value;
                const double pd = dm::stats::distance_correlation(
                    x, y, {.permutations = 199, .seed = static_cast<std::uint64_t>(r + 1)})
                                      .permutation_p_value;
                power[2 * k][l] += (pp <= 0.05) / double(reps);
                power[2 * k + 1][l] += (pd <= 0.05) / double(reps);
            }
    std::println("\n| Noise SD | Linear: Pearson | Linear: dCor | Quadratic: Pearson | "
                 "Quadratic: dCor |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t l = 0; l < levels.size(); ++l)
        std::println("| {:.2f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", levels[l],
                     power[0][l], power[1][l], power[2][l], power[3][l]);

    auto fig = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    fig.line(levels, power[0], "Linear, Pearson", {140, 182, 222}, 2.5)
        .line(levels, power[1], "Linear, dCor", {26, 90, 147}, 3.5)
        .line(levels, power[2], "Quadratic, Pearson", {232, 165, 135}, 2.5)
        .line(levels, power[3], "Quadratic, dCor", {176, 72, 43}, 3.5)
        .abline_h(0.05, {170, 170, 170})
        .y_limits(0, 1.3)
        .title("Power of independence tests (n = 50, 5% level)")
        .x_label("Noise standard deviation")
        .y_label("Rejection rate")
        .size(640, 410);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    fig.save_svg("build/distance_correlation.svg");
}
```
