| n = 30, dimension 50 each | 0.932 | 0.0021 | 0.050 | 0.057 |

| Noise SD | Linear: Pearson | Linear: dCor | Quadratic: Pearson | Quadratic: dCor |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.10 | 1.000 | 1.000 | 0.113 | 1.000 |
| 0.20 | 1.000 | 1.000 | 0.140 | 0.997 |
| 0.35 | 1.000 | 1.000 | 0.073 | 0.767 |
| 0.50 | 1.000 | 1.000 | 0.077 | 0.400 |
| 0.75 | 1.000 | 1.000 | 0.067 | 0.203 |
| 1.00 | 0.963 | 0.937 | 0.053 | 0.130 |
| 1.50 | 0.730 | 0.660 | 0.057 | 0.063 |
| 2.00 | 0.543 | 0.473 | 0.067 | 0.073 |
\image(figures/distance_correlation.svg)
// result_end

For a linear signal, distance correlation is nearly as good as Pearson: both reject in every sample at n = 100, and at n = 50 the dCor permutation test loses only 3–7 points of power as the noise grows. For anything non-monotone, Pearson and Spearman are blind. The quadratic, the circle and the variance-only dependence all have zero correlation, while distance correlation rejects in 100%, 59% and 41% of samples. The circle and the two-period sine are the hard cases for dCor too: dependence that is local rather than global is diluted when averaged over all pairs of distances.

Raw dCor is not centred at zero under independence. It averages 0.16 for n = 100 and 0.31 for n = 30, and 0.93 for 50-dimensional independent vectors with n = 30, so its value alone cannot be read as strength of dependence. The bias-corrected \(\mathcal R^*_n\) averages zero in all three cases, and is the estimate to report.

The permutation test holds its level in every setting. The t test is designed for high dimension and is accurate there (5.7% at dimension 50), but it is somewhat liberal for univariate data (6.9% at n = 30, 7.0% at n = 100). It earns its place in the circle example, where it has more power (87% against 59%), but with univariate data its edge partly reflects that excess size.

There is a final warning in the power table. At low noise the Pearson t test rejects 11–14% of quadratic samples, although the population correlation is exactly zero. The t test assumes independence, not merely zero correlation, so it is not a valid test of "no linear association" when the data are dependent in other ways.

>> Practical guidance

- Use distance correlation when the form of dependence is unknown, or when x and y are vectors of different dimensions. Report the bias-corrected estimate, and test with the permutation test (at least 999 replicates for p-values near 0.01).
- Use the t test for high-dimensional data (dozens of coordinates or more), where it is fast and accurate. For low dimension, prefer the permutation test.
- The default exponent `index` = 1 suits most uses. Smaller exponents downweight large distances, adding robustness to outliers and heavy tails, which matters because the moments behind dCor need not exist.
- For a known, monotone relationship, Pearson or Spearman correlation is more interpretable and slightly more powerful. Use dCor as a screen, then model the dependence it finds.
- The direct computation needs O(n²) memory and time. Beyond a few thousand observations, subsample or use an O(n log n) univariate algorithm.

>> References

- G. J. Székely, M. L. Rizzo and N. K. Bakirov (2007). Measuring and testing dependence by correlation of distances. *Annals of Statistics* 35(6), 2769-2794. doi:10.1214/009053607000000505
- G. J. Székely and M. L. Rizzo (2013). The distance correlation t-test of independence in high dimension. *Journal of Multivariate Analysis* 117, 193-213. doi:10.1016/j.jmva.2013.02.012
- G. J. Székely and M. L. Rizzo (2014). Partial distance correlation with methods for dissimilarities. *Annals of Statistics* 42(6), 2382-2412. doi:10.1214/14-AOS1255
- M. L. Rizzo and G. J. Székely (2022). energy: E-Statistics: Multivariate Inference via the Energy of Data. R package version 1.7.
- N. Simon and R. Tibshirani (2014). Comment on "Detecting novel associations in large data sets". arXiv:1401.7645.
